1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Hello everyone, let's take a look at some of the problems that need to be avoided in the process of recording animation in software.

2
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,000
Here we import an OBJ to do morph target.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
After opening it in the right position, we select open at the bottom.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,500
Select morph target for the target type.

5
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,000
The morph target is the number of times we have overacted.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The smaller the number, the less the number of times we have overacted.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The faster we overact, the faster we overact.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
So when we set the morph target, try to set it a little higher.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
This can prevent some cross-interference problems caused by overacting too fast.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Also, in the process of morph target, if the movement is too different,

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
such as turning around, or some other problems,

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
it is best to turn your model in the software first,

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
then do the morph target.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Don't make too much of a difference.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Next, we can see that after we have finished the movement,

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
the model's right hand is more stable.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
But if we turn to the left hand of the model,

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
we will find that it is a little unstable.

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The reason why it is unstable is because of the cross-interference between the model's hand and chest.

20
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
When there is a cross-interference in the model,

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
there is also a cross-interference in the model's outer panel,

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
because the distance between them is very close.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The fabric itself is relatively thin, and the tight position may be fine,

24
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
but in places where there are more fabric piled up,

25
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
its instability will be more obvious,

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
because there are already a lot of grid here.

27
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Plus, their collision distance now becomes very close,

28
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
and it is easy to cause instability.

29
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,000
In such a situation,

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
it may be easier to solve some more tight panels.

31
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
You can fix it directly through the fixing needle,

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
fix it on the virtual model,

33
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and force it to be fixed in that position.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
But if there is a lot of fabric,

35
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
there is no way to use the function of fixing it to the virtual model.

36
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
In general, we recommend that you try not to let the movement have some cross-interference.

37
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Because once there is a cross-interference,

38
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
you separate the movement from the fabric itself.

39
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It also has a calculation here.

40
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
So before the official recording starts,

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
make sure your model is in the process of animation recording.

42
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Otherwise, there may be a cross-interference in the process of animation recording.

43
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Next, let's look at the second case.

44
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
In this case,

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
we will see that after opening the animation editor,

46
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
when dragging the progress bar at the bottom,

47
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
this model will pass to the ground.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
In our software,

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
there will be conflicts on the ground under default conditions.

50
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
It is to prevent the panel from falling to the ground below.

51
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
In this case,

52
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
if we continue to maintain the conflicts on the ground,

53
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
then the panel will definitely be forced above the ground.

54
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Therefore, the clothing looks like there is a breakdown.

55
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Then we need to set up the conflicts on the ground.

56
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
In the background of the 3D space,

57
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
right-click and select the model attribute.

58
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Open the ground in the attribute editor.

59
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The ground is randomly selected conflicts.

60
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
When you select a conflict,

61
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
we can set its overall ground height to negative.

62
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Then we have the position of the conflict below the ground.

63
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Then you can do a test in advance.

64
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Use a piece of board to simulate

65
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
and see its final height.

66
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Next, let's look at another case.

67
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
In this case,

68
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
I have done some details of the clothes in advance.

69
