101
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Next, let's take a look at

102
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
the process of recording the animation.

103
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Sometimes we may need to

104
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
make some changes to the costume.

105
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
In the process of making some changes,

106
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
you need to avoid some problems.

107
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Sometimes, when we started recording the animation,

108
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
we didn't find any problems.

109
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
After we recorded the animation to a certain number of frames,

110
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
we found that there were some differences

111
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
or unstable places in the version.

112
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Some friends may change it

113
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
under this number of frames.

114
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
You will find that if you add some

115
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
false frames or edit the version,

116
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
the animation recorded before

117
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
will be gone.

118
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
This is because our animation

119
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
is moving along the grid.

120
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
So in the process of recording the animation

121
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
to a certain number of frames,

122
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
if you change the costume,

123
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
due to the lack of the costume animation,

124
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
when we drag the progress bar

125
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
back to the first frame,

126
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
the previous action and clothes

127
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
will not match.

128
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
First, if you accidentally encounter

129
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
such a problem,

130
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
please pause the simulation

131
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and then save the current action

132
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
of this OBJ model.

133
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
After the first frame,

134
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
you can start to edit your costume.

135
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
This means that we need to

136
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
start recording the animation again from the beginning.

137
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
In addition to the false frame

138
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
that will cause the animation recorded before to fail,

139
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
if you change the grid peak

140
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
and do some other operations,

141
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
there may be such an animation loss problem.

142
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
So in the process of recording the animation,

143
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
you must remember to go back to the initial frame

144
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
and continue to edit.

145
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Next, let's take a look at

146
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
what common operations

147
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
can be done.

148
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
First of all, these functions

149
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
will usually cause the grid to reset,

150
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
including, but not least,

151
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
modifying the board,

152
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
modifying the line,

153
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
adjusting the particle diameter,

154
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
and increasing the false frame

155
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and adjusting the rendering thickness.

156
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Note that this is to adjust the rendering thickness

157
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
rather than the collision thickness,

158
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
because the collision thickness

159
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
